WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.419
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4557.

2
00:00:01.419 --> 00:00:02.838
Begin Session III.]

3
00:00:02.838 --> 00:00:03.311
<v JENNIFER ABRAHAM CRAMER>Ready?

4
00:00:03.311 --> 00:00:04.257
Alright, wonderful.

5
00:00:04.257 --> 00:00:08.041
Okay, so today is January the twenty-eighth, 2016.

6
00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:17.500
This is Jennifer Abraham Cramer with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State Universities] Libraries,

7
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:27.432
and we are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum on a project to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

8
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:33.108
And this is interview session number three with Mr. Edward Butch Lemelle.

9
00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:35.000
<v EDWARD BUTCH LEMELLE JR.>That is he.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.069
<v CRAMER>That is he.

11
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.990
And we are here today in his house in Sulphur, Louisiana.

12
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:46.762
And your wife is also present, so can we get you to state your full name for the record?

13
00:00:46.762 --> 00:00:48.545
What is your full name?

14
00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:49.257
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Leona Lemelle.

15
00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:49.614
<v CRAMER>Okay.

16
00:00:49.614 --> 00:00:51.396
What was your maiden name?

17
00:00:51.396 --> 00:00:52.109
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Rideau, R-I-D-E-A-U.

18
00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:52.465
<v CRAMER>Okay.

19
00:00:52.465 --> 00:00:53.178
Very good.

20
00:00:53.178 --> 00:01:01.376
Okay, so it’s Leona, and Butch and I, and Rebecca is the audio engineer, and Chelsea is over in the corner as well.

21
00:01:01.376 --> 00:01:04.584
So we got a big group over here today.

22
00:01:04.584 --> 00:01:10.287
So what we're doing is we're picking up from interview number two where we left off.

23
00:01:10.287 --> 00:01:11.000
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

24
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.042
<v CRAMER>And we're going to talk . . . We've got a lot of different topics to discuss,

25
00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:22.220
but I still have some questions from listening to the second interview,

26
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:32.578
and just some things you were talking about, and I wanted to see if you could elaborate a little bit on some of them.

27
00:01:32.578 --> 00:01:40.777
So in your last interview you talked about how in the '40s and the '50s leaders . . . about how leaders,

28
00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:45.524
mostly elders and men, would get together to decide political actions.

29
00:01:45.524 --> 00:01:51.135
For example, voting for one person for . . . you gave the example of sheriff,

30
00:01:51.135 --> 00:01:59.334
and then how everybody would get together and be on one card, and that men would make the decision.

31
00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:08.397
And so I was just curious as to how did people . . . how did the men come to those kind of decisions?

32
00:02:08.397 --> 00:02:12.712
Did, you know, what kind of decisions they would be.

33
00:02:12.712 --> 00:02:20.049
<v LEMELLE>Well as I can remember one of the descendants of some of the former founders of Mossville,

34
00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:27.817
the first families, were the Rigmaidens, the Mosses, and some of that generation that fell beneath that generation.

35
00:02:27.817 --> 00:02:35.153
Especially . . . there was one, Mr. Josh Rigmaiden, who we considered more like the little mayor of Mossville.

36
00:02:35.153 --> 00:02:42.058
And there was a Mr. Dallas Moss, which was a descendant of the original Moss family.

37
00:02:42.058 --> 00:02:51.121
Well either at the . . . Mr. Josh Rigmaiden's place, he had a little business, Rigmaiden’s Grocery, and of course Mount Zion Church.

38
00:02:51.121 --> 00:02:59.752
Mr. Moss, Dallas Moss, was probably one of the old deacons there at the church, some of the first members.

39
00:02:59.752 --> 00:03:09.677
<c.p>And these were the kind of men that got together, and they discussed . . . hate to use the word politics, but they discussed leadership.

40
00:03:09.677 --> 00:03:13.561
Who would be best fitted for our little community.

41
00:03:13.561 --> 00:03:17.014
Who would fill the needs of the community?

42
00:03:17.014 --> 00:03:24.782
And once they discussed that among themselves, a few of the men of the community and the church,

43
00:03:24.782 --> 00:03:33.845
well this is when they would call—then they were called the “body” of the community leaders and families together and say,

44
00:03:33.845 --> 00:03:37.297
"This is what we would like to do.

45
00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:42.476
This is who we think would be best fitted for our sheriff.

46
00:03:42.476 --> 00:03:46.791
Sheriff Ham Reed, we think this is who we need.

47
00:03:46.791 --> 00:03:55.422
We think that,” I guess I can call names, “that Mr. Frank Salter was best fitted for our district attorney.”

48
00:03:55.422 --> 00:03:59.306
And it was a unanimous decision that was made.

49
00:03:59.306 --> 00:04:03.622
Usually a hundred percent of the community went that way.

50
00:04:03.622 --> 00:04:06.643
Even things affiliated with the Mossville School.

51
00:04:06.643 --> 00:04:11.390
The men then were leaders, and the women were followers. [Laughs]

52
00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:17.000
<v CRAMER>You say that while looking over at Leona like, "Uh oh." [Laughs]

53
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.644
<v LEMELLE>But, in all sincerity,

54
00:04:18.644 --> 00:04:30.562
the men in those day in the '40s and the '50s they made . . . I guess they made the decisions for the community that were best suited for the community.

55
00:04:30.562 --> 00:04:34.260
<v CRAMER>So what would the needs of the community be?

56
00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:38.781
Can you think of some examples of what . . . How, for example,

57
00:04:38.781 --> 00:04:41.658
Ham Reed or . . . I forget, Frank Salter . . .

58
00:04:41.658 --> 00:04:42.479
<v LEMELLE>Frank Salter.

59
00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:47.000
<v CRAMER>. . . like how could they help with those type of needs?

60
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.702
<v LEMELLE>Well in any given community or any area where there was families,

61
00:04:52.702 --> 00:04:59.831
there was also . . . between your school and families there was also a need of discipline.

62
00:04:59.831 --> 00:05:05.058
And if young men or young women got into certain situations,

63
00:05:05.058 --> 00:05:17.413
then they knew that Mr. Ham Reed would assist in that particular young person's life, and not to just take them and slam them in jail.

64
00:05:17.413 --> 00:05:28.343
I guess that was one of the biggest things is that we had people that the community leaders could go to and say,

65
00:05:28.343 --> 00:05:32.145
"Well this is Mr. Lemelle's son, and yes,

66
00:05:32.145 --> 00:05:41.649
he made a mistake so let's see what kind of way we can correct him besides putting him behind bars."

67
00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:44.975
There's other means that we could take.

68
00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:55.430
Put him on the football field and make him run, or make him come over to Mr. Rigmaiden's store and clean up.

69
00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.756
We had community service in those days.

70
00:05:58.756 --> 00:06:03.983
They just coming up with it, but we had community services.

71
00:06:03.983 --> 00:06:13.012
You may have to go and from your punishment might have been to go clean somebody's yard, or whatever.

72
00:06:13.012 --> 00:06:17.289
But this is most of what the men were,

73
00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:26.318
with people in leadership as far as like the sheriff's department, the district attorney, school board, all these things.

74
00:06:26.318 --> 00:06:36.298
Those men paid close attention to see that we had, I guess, a way of letting leadership fall into the community.

75
00:06:36.298 --> 00:06:42.000
They would appoint even men as like little deputies and stuff.

76
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.329
In fact, when I was in high school one of our school bus drivers was Mr. J.C.

77
00:06:49.329 --> 00:06:52.347
Hamilton who moved us to Mossville community.

78
00:06:52.347 --> 00:06:58.383
And he played a big part because Mr. Hamilton was a school bus driver,

79
00:06:58.383 --> 00:07:07.868
but he worked under them as a . . . I can't think of what it was, but it was more like a little policeman.

80
00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:13.473
And he had his little badge, and of course, he had his gun.

81
00:07:13.473 --> 00:07:16.060
Civil defense, that's what it was.

82
00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:20.371
Civil defense fell under the leadership of the sheriff's department.

83
00:07:20.371 --> 00:07:22.958
He was with the civil defense.

84
00:07:22.958 --> 00:07:33.737
He was very active with the young people because he was the bus driver, but he'd taken us on trips wherever we had to go.

85
00:07:33.737 --> 00:07:41.066
And of course, from Mossville school up to [Goodly?] Road you didn't . . . you stayed out of trouble.

86
00:07:41.066 --> 00:07:41.497
Everybody

87
00:07:41.497 --> 00:07:46.240
<c.p>knew you was going to get it when you got in.

88
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.257
Somebody was paying attention to the kids.

89
00:07:49.257 --> 00:07:52.275
And the authority was given to them.

90
00:07:52.275 --> 00:07:54.000
They that authority.

91
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.548
<v CRAMER>So a couple of other questions while you were talking, came to mind.

92
00:07:59.548 --> 00:08:05.096
One is, what about the laws that weren't necessarily fair that were broken?

93
00:08:05.096 --> 00:08:09.364
So for example, Jim Crow laws or anything like that.

94
00:08:09.364 --> 00:08:17.046
So what if somebody got arrested for something that today wouldn't even be lawful to arrest somebody for?

95
00:08:17.046 --> 00:08:23.021
I mean, I know that's kind of a leading question and I apologize but . . .

96
00:08:23.021 --> 00:08:29.423
<v LEMELLE>Well I'm almost say some of the . . . Jim Crow, man that was a hard one.

97
00:08:29.423 --> 00:08:35.397
Because back, to me, young people, we didn't get into too many serious things.

98
00:08:35.397 --> 00:08:38.385
It was always little old minor things,

99
00:08:38.385 --> 00:08:48.628
and there was some things that happened and it came back down to . . . with adults that it came back down to who you knew.

100
00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:52.469
There were gofers . . . go forth and speak for you.

101
00:08:52.469 --> 00:09:03.138
A lot of people are shy of the idea that a little community like this how many murders we've had here in the Mossville community.

102
00:09:03.138 --> 00:09:09.113
There was a few, but a lot of times the men . . . Again I say,

103
00:09:09.113 --> 00:09:13.808
like Mr. Josh Rigmaiden, would go and plead this person's case.

104
00:09:13.808 --> 00:09:24.477
Unless it was just purely out of . . . I know several people that what you got away with then for such a short period of time,

105
00:09:24.477 --> 00:09:28.745
of jail time, you would do some serious time now.

106
00:09:28.745 --> 00:09:31.305
You would do some life or . . .

107
00:09:31.305 --> 00:09:33.866
<v CRAMER>Like negligent homicide kind of thing?

108
00:09:33.866 --> 00:09:34.293
<v LEMELLE>Right.

109
00:09:34.293 --> 00:09:35.146
<v CRAMER>Like that?

110
00:09:35.146 --> 00:09:36.000
<v LEMELLE>Right.

111
00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.530
<v CRAMER>Like if there's a fight . . .

112
00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:39.036
<v LEMELLE>Fight.

113
00:09:39.036 --> 00:09:41.060
<v CRAMER>Breaks out and somebody . . .

114
00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:47.639
<v LEMELLE>Breaks out and someone gets shot or get cut or stabbed to death.

115
00:09:47.639 --> 00:09:51.687
That's the best I can remember on that.

116
00:09:51.687 --> 00:09:56.241
<v CRAMER>Well my other questions are, so why all men?

117
00:09:56.241 --> 00:10:04.337
What . . . You kind of jokingly said sort of . . . You had a big smile on your face.

118
00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:06.867
You said, "The women followed."

119
00:10:06.867 --> 00:10:08.892
So what was it?

120
00:10:08.892 --> 00:10:16.482
Was that just the times, or what role did women play in these big decisions?

121
00:10:16.482 --> 00:10:24.578
<v LEMELLE>I would think to say that, my way of thinking, the women played a great part,

122
00:10:24.578 --> 00:10:35.205
because I would say ninety percent of the women that I can remember when I was coming up was either Sanctified,

123
00:10:35.205 --> 00:10:40.265
Baptist . . . Ninety percent of the community that's what it was.

124
00:10:40.265 --> 00:10:46.337
And the women then, they fought for right according to God's word.

125
00:10:46.337 --> 00:10:53.928
So they played a big part in the church and brought that to the home.

126
00:10:53.928 --> 00:10:58.482
So women . . . My mom was a pretty solid lady.

127
00:10:58.482 --> 00:11:09.108
A lot of things that we would have gotten away with my father, but we didn't get away with the mother.

128
00:11:09.108 --> 00:11:14.675
<c.p>And the mothers and the women, kept their homes in order.

129
00:11:14.675 --> 00:11:16.699
They kept their communities.

130
00:11:16.699 --> 00:11:20.241
Some of the strongest educators were women.

131
00:11:20.241 --> 00:11:32.386
I would think that when I was in high school eighty percent of the instructors were women, and this was women that didn't play.

132
00:11:32.386 --> 00:11:34.410
You met Ms. Rogers.

133
00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.434
Always mention her name.

134
00:11:36.434 --> 00:11:40.482
And there was others: Ms. Paige, Ms. Cole,

135
00:11:40.482 --> 00:11:50.507
they had . . . So I'm saying in the community to me it started with the women first of all in the church,

136
00:11:50.507 --> 00:11:56.775
their beliefs and their fear of God, and they brought this to the home.

137
00:11:56.775 --> 00:12:02.596
And then husbands and wives would sit down and come to an agreement.

138
00:12:02.596 --> 00:12:12.894
And this is man's law and this is God's law, so we put them together see what we going to come up with.

139
00:12:12.894 --> 00:12:23.192
Man say this, and God say this, so we put it together and we going to make this thing work for the kids.

140
00:12:23.192 --> 00:12:28.564
That was the biggest thing, I think, when I was coming up.

141
00:12:28.564 --> 00:12:30.803
Everybody provided for the kids.

142
00:12:30.803 --> 00:12:33.937
It was all about children coming up.

143
00:12:33.937 --> 00:12:37.071
You didn't make mistakes down the road.

144
00:12:37.071 --> 00:12:41.549
Everyone was . . . Every parent was responsible for someone else kid.

145
00:12:41.549 --> 00:12:45.131
It taken a village to raise the kids.

146
00:12:45.131 --> 00:12:48.265
This is what Mossville was all about.

147
00:12:48.265 --> 00:13:00.801
Sometime I talk about it and I get . . . I don't know if I just get full in memories of how it was being . . . I use the word tutor.

148
00:13:00.801 --> 00:13:03.488
Being tutored by so many adults.

149
00:13:03.488 --> 00:13:06.174
Everybody was concerned about everybody's child.

150
00:13:06.174 --> 00:13:18.263
My mother's home, I can't tell you how many young men came through that house that if I picked up the phone now and called them say,

151
00:13:18.263 --> 00:13:24.979
"Oh man, I remember when we did such and such thing at Big Mama's house.

152
00:13:24.979 --> 00:13:28.113
I remember that Sunday man, you know."

153
00:13:28.113 --> 00:13:37.963
Not only my mother's, but I can speak that because I know, but I know other guys that I grew up with.

154
00:13:37.963 --> 00:13:40.650
I have a dear, dear friend.

155
00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.441
He lives in Florida.

156
00:13:42.441 --> 00:13:44.679
His name was [Leeman Shanker?].

157
00:13:44.679 --> 00:13:50.948
We still yet communicate by phone at least two or three times a week.

158
00:13:50.948 --> 00:14:00.798
His father was my Cub Scout leader, and we still talk about those days when we all gathered at Mr. Shanker's house,

159
00:14:00.798 --> 00:14:06.618
we all gathered at Mount Zion church and different ones would come in.

160
00:14:06.618 --> 00:14:08.857
Mr. McKeever Edwards was a

161
00:14:08.857 --> 00:14:09.753
<c.p>great teacher.

162
00:14:09.753 --> 00:14:18.260
And like I say, sometimes I think about the walks that I had from my school to my home.

163
00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:23.632
The people that paid attention to you and what you were doing.

164
00:14:23.632 --> 00:14:25.871
I guess it brings tears.

165
00:14:25.871 --> 00:14:32.587
My wife would tell you how much I'm glad that we . . . God provided for us,

166
00:14:32.587 --> 00:14:38.855
but it was a hurt for me to have to give up my community.

167
00:14:38.855 --> 00:14:43.780
But I knew it was nothing I could do about it.

168
00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.915
But just the old memories, oh man.

169
00:14:46.915 --> 00:15:04.376
We have some memories that if I could share every memory I had with people and let them realize why this is just so important to me is the oral history of my grandkids and great grandkids can say,

170
00:15:04.376 --> 00:15:07.510
"Well this is what Paw-Paw came from.

171
00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.645
This is why he's where he's at."

172
00:15:10.645 --> 00:15:11.988
Mossville, just Mossville.

173
00:15:11.988 --> 00:15:16.465
<v CRAMER>Painting that picture, that's what we're really trying to do.

174
00:15:16.465 --> 00:15:20.047
Now this is totally and completely jumping ahead,

175
00:15:20.047 --> 00:15:28.106
but I figure while we're kind of on the subject I'm very curious to know . . . You're here now.

176
00:15:28.106 --> 00:15:29.449
You're in Sulphur.

177
00:15:29.449 --> 00:15:32.136
You've moved from Mossville to Sulphur.

178
00:15:32.136 --> 00:15:35.270
What is the community of Mossville like?

179
00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:46.911
And by that I sort of mean are you able to keep up with people on a regular basis with whom you used to be neighbors,

180
00:15:46.911 --> 00:15:50.941
go to church, and if so kind of how.

181
00:15:50.941 --> 00:15:59.000
I know it's a long convoluted question, but just how is that going for you right now?

182
00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.657
<v LEMELLE>And I'm always on a percentage.

183
00:16:01.657 --> 00:16:08.743
If I taken a hundred percent of the people that I knew and lived in Mossville,

184
00:16:08.743 --> 00:16:17.600
I guess we're down to twenty percent that we communicate with daily, weekly, I have in my community left there.

185
00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:25.571
My family from the Towners and the Lemelles, I only have two of my families that's still there.

186
00:16:25.571 --> 00:16:26.900
That's two sisters.

187
00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.557
My niece, nephews, they already gone.

188
00:16:29.557 --> 00:16:34.871
And out of those . . . I'm just going to put it like this.

189
00:16:34.871 --> 00:16:41.071
My mother had six kids, twenty or thirty grands, and so many great-great grands,

190
00:16:41.071 --> 00:16:51.700
and out of all of those I think we have maybe four families that . . . I have two sisters and a couple of nieces and

191
00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:54.357
<c.p>nephews that we talk to frequently.

192
00:16:54.357 --> 00:17:00.114
And the closest one I have is a first cousin, Mr. [Artis Ledoux?].

193
00:17:00.114 --> 00:17:02.329
We communicate all the time.

194
00:17:02.329 --> 00:17:09.857
But on a scale from one to ten, I'd say we about two that we communicate with.

195
00:17:09.857 --> 00:17:16.943
Half of the people that was my neighbors, I don't even know where they are now.

196
00:17:16.943 --> 00:17:18.271
I really don't.

197
00:17:18.271 --> 00:17:24.914
I have a first cousin that lived across the street from me behind the church.

198
00:17:24.914 --> 00:17:28.014
I don't even know where he's at.

199
00:17:28.014 --> 00:17:32.000
<v CRAMER>So you don't see them at church or . . .?

200
00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.524
<v LEMELLE>He doesn't go to our church.

201
00:17:34.524 --> 00:17:37.890
He lived . . . His home was behind the church.

202
00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:39.572
His name was [Carey?].

203
00:17:39.572 --> 00:17:47.986
This was the guy that say he wants to give an oral history of what his thing was about Mossville.

204
00:17:47.986 --> 00:17:49.248
He [ . . .?] and stuff.

205
00:17:49.248 --> 00:17:53.034
He . . . I don't even . . . I saw him . . . What, Sunday?

206
00:17:53.034 --> 00:17:53.455
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Carey?

207
00:17:53.455 --> 00:17:55.559
<v LEMELLE>A couple of Sundays ago?

208
00:17:55.559 --> 00:17:58.083
No, I saw him for Christmas.

209
00:17:58.083 --> 00:17:59.766
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, because that was . . .

210
00:17:59.766 --> 00:18:03.972
<v LEMELLE>We had a Christmas dinner, the Lemelles, and he came.

211
00:18:03.972 --> 00:18:04.393
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

212
00:18:04.393 --> 00:18:09.862
<v LEMELLE>And I haven't seen . . . I'm just saying people that I saw every day.

213
00:18:09.862 --> 00:18:17.014
