<v CRAMER>Right.

438
00:30:27.707 --> 00:30:32.466
And sometimes people who have Alzheimer’s or dementia remember the past well.

439
00:30:32.466 --> 00:30:33.259
<v LEMELLE>The past.

440
00:30:33.259 --> 00:30:33.655
Well.

441
00:30:33.655 --> 00:30:40.000
<v CRAMER>So that's . . . I mean, I think if we could get him at a good time.

442
00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.966
<v LEMELLE>I'm going to check with Judy and see.

443
00:30:42.966 --> 00:30:44.078
<v CRAMER>Okay, that’s great.

444
00:30:44.078 --> 00:30:44.819
Thank you.

445
00:30:44.819 --> 00:30:46.302
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Where does he live?

446
00:30:46.302 --> 00:30:47.043
<v LEMELLE>To what?

447
00:30:47.043 --> 00:30:48.526
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Where do John live?

448
00:30:48.526 --> 00:30:51.121
<v LEMELLE>Mr. John is in Moss Bluff, baby.

449
00:30:51.121 --> 00:30:58.905
<v CRAMER>So I think we talked about . . . I was going to ask this again, but I think we already talked about it.

450
00:30:58.905 --> 00:31:04.466
You had mentioned . . . you said that the community, around the '60s, the community lost leadership.

451
00:31:04.466 --> 00:31:09.284
And you kind of mentioned changes in government, younger generations became more radical,

452
00:31:09.284 --> 00:31:15.216
things just sort of changed in the '60s, and that there was a loss of leadership.

453
00:31:15.216 --> 00:31:20.405
I was wondering if there was anything specific you wanted to add to that.

454
00:31:20.405 --> 00:31:23.000
Did the loss of leadership last?

455
00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:28.556
<v LEMELLE>I would say the latter part of the '60s.

456
00:31:28.556 --> 00:31:33.495
Because the early '60s was good, the latter,

457
00:31:33.495 --> 00:31:45.224
latter part of the '60s when there were changes being made in government, in the world as a whole.

458
00:31:45.224 --> 00:31:58.189
The movement in the '60s, and we all know that in the '60s there were marches that began for equal rights.

459
00:31:58.189 --> 00:32:11.770
And some of the things that was being done was against what our great Dr. Martin Luther King was preaching and teaching.

460
00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:25.352
And we saw it coming down, I guess, where we had younger people then wanted to be more of a radical act.

461
00:32:25.352 --> 00:32:33.995
Of course, I came out of school in '65 and we were totally different.

462
00:32:33.995 --> 00:32:41.403
But during that period of time from '67, '68, '69, to me,

463
00:32:41.403 --> 00:32:53.750
it was a change because it was almost that you had to do what was being done in order to . . .

464
00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:54.985
<v CRAMER>Pause it?

465
00:32:54.985 --> 00:32:57.454
We can pause this.

466
00:32:57.454 --> 00:32:59.923
<v LEMELLE>You want to pause?

467
00:32:59.923 --> 00:33:00.541
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

468
00:33:00.541 --> 00:33:02.393
<c.p>[break in tape]

469
00:33:02.393 --> 00:33:12.888
<v CRAMER>When it was on pause I asked . . . You were talking about leadership some more and I said,

470
00:33:12.888 --> 00:33:21.531
"Look, you know was it basically after Josh Rigmaiden died that the leadership changed?"

471
00:33:21.531 --> 00:33:24.000
And you said . . .

472
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:33.642
<v LEMELLE>Well it was not that we lacked leadership, but it was a different type of leadership.

473
00:33:33.642 --> 00:33:40.874
Because everything, what you know, everything in the cities or whatever, trickles

474
00:33:40.874 --> 00:33:45.695
<c.p>down to your small cities, your smallest communities.

475
00:33:45.695 --> 00:33:57.748
And it just wasn't a place for a Malcolm X type neighborhood because this is what we weren't all about.

476
00:33:57.748 --> 00:34:03.172
So leaders then became, I would say more pushy.

477
00:34:03.172 --> 00:34:18.841
I don't like to call people radical, but more pushy because they felt that’s what had to be done in order to have your voice heard.

478
00:34:18.841 --> 00:34:31.497
Examples, people heard the great Dr. Martin Luther King and the type of messages that he brought, always being strong, humble.

479
00:34:31.497 --> 00:34:44.152
But then there were others that was bringing a different message that was strong and more on the radical-type of force.

480
00:34:44.152 --> 00:34:55.000
So all this trickled down from the big city to a smaller city down to small communities.

481
00:34:55.000 --> 00:35:05.078
Mossville is not the only community in the world that had went through some of the things that we went through.

482
00:35:05.078 --> 00:35:11.796
But somehow we still try to stay focused on what the real issue was.

483
00:35:11.796 --> 00:35:14.196
What were we looking for.

484
00:35:14.196 --> 00:35:29.552
I wanted my daughter to have the same . . . and my children to have the same opportunity that any other red-blooded American had because I felt that we were citizens of a great,

485
00:35:29.552 --> 00:35:35.791
great United States, and one of the sweetest, finest communities on God's earth.

486
00:35:35.791 --> 00:35:38.190
So it wasn't a loss.

487
00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:42.509
It was a loss of the way we were.

488
00:35:42.509 --> 00:35:45.389
It was a loss in change.

489
00:35:45.389 --> 00:35:47.788
To me it's that simple.

490
00:35:47.788 --> 00:35:54.987
It was a loss in change, the way you did business, the way things did,

491
00:35:54.987 --> 00:36:04.584
the way things . . . Even in the homes people that changing in their homes because, "we have to do this because."

492
00:36:04.584 --> 00:36:06.984
No, you don't have to.

493
00:36:06.984 --> 00:36:14.182
You stay with what you . . . This is one things I've always said about my wife.

494
00:36:14.182 --> 00:36:21.381
She's always stayed firm into what she believed the way your children should have been.

495
00:36:21.381 --> 00:36:24.740
And she do it with her grandkids.

496
00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:28.099
This is the way my daddy did.

497
00:36:28.099 --> 00:36:30.499
To me it was right.

498
00:36:30.499 --> 00:36:32.418
It kept me straight.

499
00:36:32.418 --> 00:36:33.858
I didn't steal.

500
00:36:33.858 --> 00:36:35.298
I didn't lie.

501
00:36:35.298 --> 00:36:37.217
I didn't do this.

502
00:36:37.217 --> 00:36:39.617
And we respected out neighbor.

503
00:36:39.617 --> 00:36:41.536
We respected the law.

504
00:36:41.536 --> 00:36:42.976
We respected God.

505
00:36:42.976 --> 00:36:48.735
And a lot of this, we lost in our little small community.

506
00:36:48.735 --> 00:36:51.614
We lost a lot of this.

507
00:36:51.614 --> 00:36:55.933
And I'm going to tell you the reason why.

508
00:36:55.933 --> 00:36:58.332
In my opinion, by mistakes.

509
00:36:58.332 --> 00:37:02.651
Younger people start doing what they had to do.

510
00:37:02.651 --> 00:37:03.611
People from

511
00:37:03.611 --> 00:37:06.971
<c.p>other places moved in to our area.

512
00:37:06.971 --> 00:37:14.649
You called the latest name a minute ago that gave a history, she's not a Mossvillion.

513
00:37:14.649 --> 00:37:21.367
She passed through and married a Mossvillion, which did not make her a Mossvillion.

514
00:37:21.367 --> 00:37:23.287
I am a Mossvillion.

515
00:37:23.287 --> 00:37:34.804
But I thank God she became a Mossvillion with me, and she helped stay with the old rules and stay with the old way.

516
00:37:34.804 --> 00:37:37.204
That's the way it went.

517
00:37:37.204 --> 00:37:39.123
So leadership just changed.

518
00:37:39.123 --> 00:37:43.442
<v CRAMER>So during this time period Mossville was growing, then?

519
00:37:43.442 --> 00:37:50.161
So it like . . . There were different economic opportunities and that's why people were coming?

520
00:37:50.161 --> 00:37:54.000
What kind of economic activities were there?

521
00:37:54.000 --> 00:38:02.123
<v LEMELLE>Well let me put it like this, when you’re in a rural area such as Mossville,

522
00:38:02.123 --> 00:38:10.246
we were on the map with probably one of the greatest black schools in the state.

523
00:38:10.246 --> 00:38:22.431
When people were coming here Mossville High School was a school that, in the '59s, a few years, they were recognized statewide academically, sports.

524
00:38:22.431 --> 00:38:26.492
And then there were jobs that came open.

525
00:38:26.492 --> 00:38:40.200
But of course it was economical for someone to move here and pay forty thousand dollars for a house then to go somewhere else and pay ninety-five.

526
00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:46.292
So everything here was less expensive for them to move to Mossville.

527
00:38:46.292 --> 00:38:52.892
Buy them some land, build them a home, and settle in this community.

528
00:38:52.892 --> 00:38:57.462
It was less expensive than to go somewhere else.

529
00:38:57.462 --> 00:39:00.000
<v CRAMER>Where were people working?

530
00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.903
<v LEMELLE>Well a lot of people came into construction work.

531
00:39:03.903 --> 00:39:06.071
The refineries, for an example.

532
00:39:06.071 --> 00:39:08.673
I keep calling this man's name.

533
00:39:08.673 --> 00:39:13.444
His family to me was always a great family, Mr. Valery

534
00:39:13.444 --> 00:39:13.878
<c.p>Montgomery.

535
00:39:13.878 --> 00:39:22.985
I was a little boy when he and Wes and them move here from around Lafayette [Louisiana] out in the country.

536
00:39:22.985 --> 00:39:25.153
And they met my father,

537
00:39:25.153 --> 00:39:34.694
and I still say Wes will remember they came and met my father and my father loaned him his first fifty cents.

538
00:39:34.694 --> 00:39:43.801
And that's when Mr. Valery and them start working into the construction part of building the refinery or the refinery area.

539
00:39:43.801 --> 00:39:49.439
And Valery and Wes got blessed to get into Conoco as hired laborers.

540
00:39:49.439 --> 00:39:58.546
And I think Val stayed at Conoco refinery probably for thirty plus years, he and Wes, and with his other business.

541
00:39:58.546 --> 00:40:03.750
So people move here because it was a great place to come.

542
00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:14.158
And the cost of living for you wasn't extreme if you was living somewhere in Maplewood trying to go to work in Cities Service.

543
00:40:14.158 --> 00:40:16.760
<v CRAMER>What other . . . So there's Cities Service.

544
00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:18.061
There was Conoco.

545
00:40:18.061 --> 00:40:20.230
There was construction around it.

546
00:40:20.230 --> 00:40:22.832
What other places were employing people?

547
00:40:22.832 --> 00:40:24.133
<v LEMELLE>That's about it.

548
00:40:24.133 --> 00:40:25.000
<v CRAMER>Okay.

549
00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:33.439
<v LEMELLE>Those were probably your main sources of income was your refineries, which was the two major refineries: Cities Service and Conoco.

550
00:40:33.439 --> 00:40:34.645
That was before.

551
00:40:34.645 --> 00:40:39.869
This was back in the '50s and '60s that it was really booming.

552
00:40:39.869 --> 00:40:47.907
<v CRAMER>So we’re going to change gears a little bit here, because we talked about you living in Boulder, Colorado briefly.

553
00:40:47.907 --> 00:40:55.140
But we didn't talk about how long you lived there, when you came back, who you lived with.

554
00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:58.757
So I'm going to ask you all those questions.

555
00:40:58.757 --> 00:41:00.364
You said you went

556
00:41:00.364 --> 00:41:01.972
<c.p>when you were sixteen?

557
00:41:01.972 --> 00:41:04.785
So that makes . . . I'm terrible with math.

558
00:41:04.785 --> 00:41:06.393
<v LEMELLE>What year was that?

559
00:41:06.393 --> 00:41:07.196
<v CRAMER>Sixty-two, maybe?

560
00:41:07.196 --> 00:41:08.000
<v LEMELLE>Sixty-one?

561
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:08.581
<v CRAMER>Sixty-one?

562
00:41:08.581 --> 00:41:09.161
Okay.

563
00:41:09.161 --> 00:41:09.361
<v LEMELLE>Sixty . . . I'd have to call her.

564
00:41:09.161 --> 00:41:12.645
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[ . . .?]

565
00:41:12.645 --> 00:41:14.387
<v CRAMER>So early '60s.

566
00:41:14.387 --> 00:41:21.935
<v LEMELLE>Probably 1960 because I would have been like a freshman in high school.

567
00:41:21.935 --> 00:41:26.000
<v CRAMER>How long did you stay there?

568
00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:27.697
<v LEMELLE>I stayed there probably half the year.

569
00:41:27.697 --> 00:41:28.424
Went to school.

570
00:41:28.424 --> 00:41:29.152
<v CRAMER>Who did you . . .

571
00:41:29.152 --> 00:41:33.273
<v LEMELLE>I lived with . . . Back in those days we call your mother's godchild your love sister or half-sister.

572
00:41:33.273 --> 00:41:34.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh okay.

573
00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:41.562
<v LEMELLE>And in other words my mother's baby sister's daughter that my grandmother raised.

574
00:41:41.562 --> 00:41:44.471
Her name was Gwendolyn Harris.

575
00:41:44.471 --> 00:41:46.797
She married a Hardy.

576
00:41:46.797 --> 00:42:04.830
And she moved from here to Boulder in . . . I left here the summer to go and help her with her little kids because she had like four or five little kids,

577
00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:07.739
and she moved to Colorado.

578
00:42:07.739 --> 00:42:18.209
I rode the train with her to go and help her on the train with the little ones.

579
00:42:18.209 --> 00:42:22.281
Got there and began to enjoy it.

580
00:42:22.281 --> 00:42:25.190
So I started school there.

581
00:42:25.190 --> 00:42:29.261
Which was a . . . What you call it?

582
00:42:29.261 --> 00:42:33.915
It was like a . . . Not a climate change.

583
00:42:33.915 --> 00:42:38.569
It was a climate change, but environmental change.

584
00:42:38.569 --> 00:42:40.314
I speak openly.

585
00:42:40.314 --> 00:42:50.784
You are raised by a mother, father, with five sisters in a one hundred percent black community, segregated.

586
00:42:50.784 --> 00:43:07.072
You just left a place that you had to go to the back door to use the bathroom or you looked at a sign that said, "Whites only.”

587
00:43:07.072 --> 00:43:07.654
“Coloreds.”

588
00:43:07.654 --> 00:43:22.196
I paid the same amount for a hamburger that everyone else did, but I had to go to the back window to get a hamburger.

589
00:43:22.196 --> 00:43:32.085
I left that here, and when I got there it was like I'm in a different world.

590
00:43:32.085 --> 00:43:47.791
I can't believe that I have friends that I can walk in . . . This was my first time ever going into a Taco Bell in the front door,

591
00:43:47.791 --> 00:43:51.281
and went in and sat down.

592
00:43:51.281 --> 00:43:55.935
I didn't even know what a taco was.

593
00:43:55.935 --> 00:44:02.915
And the guy say . . . We're ordering tacos and I'm dumb to that.

594
00:44:02.915 --> 00:44:06.405
I said, "I'll have the same."

595
00:44:06.405 --> 00:44:11.059
I didn't . . . I'm used to rice and gravy.

596
00:44:11.059 --> 00:44:20.948
But it was like a total . . . Going from a dark room into a room that's lit up.

597
00:44:20.948 --> 00:44:26.765
There's another world that I never seen . . . and totally different.

598
00:44:26.765 --> 00:44:32.000
Boulder, Colorado was totally different from Mossville, Louisiana.

599
00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:34.219
<v CRAMER>So people treated you differently?

600
00:44:34.219 --> 00:44:35.551
<v LEMELLE>In which way?

601
00:44:35.551 --> 00:44:39.545
<v CRAMER>Well I mean, I guess society treating everyone differently.

602
00:44:39.545 --> 00:44:44.428
<v LEMELLE>Society there was like . . . the only thing white here is snow.

603
00:44:44.428 --> 00:44:49.310
The only thing black here is the roof of your house.

604
00:44:49.310 --> 00:44:52.861
As far as you, you are a person.

605
00:44:52.861 --> 00:44:53.305
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

606
00:44:53.305 --> 00:44:55.080
You're just a person.

607
00:44:55.080 --> 00:44:56.856
<v LEMELLE>You are a person.

608
00:44:56.856 --> 00:45:01.738
And my high school, Boulder High School . . . one, two, three, four,

609
00:45:01.738 --> 00:45:10.171
five . . . my freshman class was over 1,500 students, and out of that class I was the fifth African American.

610
00:45:10.171 --> 00:45:11.947
They had two girls.

611
00:45:11.947 --> 00:45:13.278
They were twins.

612
00:45:13.278 --> 00:45:15.941
They had a friend of mine.

613
00:45:15.941 --> 00:45:19.936
His name was Buddy [Iniss?] and [Flowers?] and myself.

614
00:45:19.936 --> 00:45:22.155
So I was number six.

615
00:45:22.155 --> 00:45:25.706
I've always been number six for some reason.

616
00:45:25.706 --> 00:45:28.369
Mama had five daughters and six.

617
00:45:28.369 --> 00:45:35.471
So this was the friends, but everyone at Boulder High School were super, super good people.

618
00:45:35.471 --> 00:45:41.241
Like I said, it wasn't no . . . I didn't run into a color barrier.

619
00:45:41.241 --> 00:45:49.674
But it was such a big change for me, so it almost put you into a state of shock.

620
00:45:49.674 --> 00:45:51.893
What am I doing here?

621
00:45:51.893 --> 00:45:55.000
I'm going to make it here.

622
00:45:55.000 --> 00:46:02.524
<v CRAMER>Did people talk about racial politics openly, or was it just an understood thing?

623
00:46:02.524 --> 00:46:13.272
<v LEMELLE>It was understood that some of the . . . Because we lived . . . I lived . . . I never forget the street was Water Canal.

624
00:46:13.272 --> 00:46:24.020
We lived not far from the college . . . from Boulder . . . my school, Boulder High, and then there was CU [Colorado University].

625
00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:32.082
We could walk from our place to CU and I'm used to seeing football players.

626
00:46:32.082 --> 00:46:39.068
I went to . . . I left for school that . . . My peers were all black,

627
00:46:39.068 --> 00:46:44.980
and the college that I saw, Grambling, Southern, were all black.

628
00:46:44.980 --> 00:46:55.728
I'm going to a college now that, it was just . . . This guy I met, he was from McKinley High School.

629
00:46:55.728 --> 00:46:56.803
Yes, ma'am.

630
00:46:56.803 --> 00:47:03.789
He got a scholarship at CU One of the first people I met.

631
00:47:03.789 --> 00:47:05.939
I was shooting basketball.

632
00:47:05.939 --> 00:47:11.313
He was from McKinley High School around Baton Rouge [Louisiana].

633
00:47:11.313 --> 00:47:14.000
<v CRAMER>What was his name?

634
00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:16.985
<v LEMELLE>All I can remember, we call him Tank.

635
00:47:16.985 --> 00:47:20.343
He was . . . I mean, he was like a superman.

636
00:47:20.343 --> 00:47:26.685
And when this dude got a football in his hand it was like . . . And he ran track.

637
00:47:26.685 --> 00:47:28.177
And he had calves.

638
00:47:28.177 --> 00:47:31.908
Man, I looked and I said, "Man, where you from?"

639
00:47:31.908 --> 00:47:33.401
He called me Red.

640
00:47:33.401 --> 00:47:36.012
He said, "Where you from, Little Red?"

641
00:47:36.012 --> 00:47:38.251
I said, "Man, I'm from Louisiana."

642
00:47:38.251 --> 00:47:40.862
He said, "Little Red, I'm from Louisiana."

643
00:47:40.862 --> 00:47:41.982
I said, "What?"

644
00:47:41.982 --> 00:47:46.459
I said, "I'm from a little place called Mossville, Mossville High School."

645
00:47:46.459 --> 00:47:47.578
"Oh," he said.

646
00:47:47.578 --> 00:47:50.190
"I ran over them boys down there.

647
00:47:50.190 --> 00:47:52.055
I'm from McKinley High School."

648
00:47:52.055 --> 00:47:54.667
I said, "You've got to be kidding."

649
00:47:54.667 --> 00:47:59.517
So we got to be . . . Now he was like a freshman in college.

650
00:47:59.517 --> 00:48:03.621
I'm still a kid, and he tucked me under his wing.

651
00:48:03.621 --> 00:48:10.709
And say he was . . . I can’t think of that kid's first name but all they called him was Tank.

652
00:48:10.709 --> 00:48:14.813
But to see here's a college that you're looking at majority,

653
00:48:14.813 --> 00:48:21.902
but you had black and white playing ball together and loving each other, and just getting along like family.

654
00:48:21.902 --> 00:48:26.752
And it was like, you from California, you know what I'm talking about.

655
00:48:26.752 --> 00:48:29.737
So leaving the South and going there was

656
00:48:29.737 --> 00:48:31.229
<c.p>a life change experience.

657
00:48:31.229 --> 00:48:36.453
But being Mama's baby and stuff I couldn't stay away long enough to me.

658
00:48:36.453 --> 00:48:38.318
I wish I would have.

659
00:48:38.318 --> 00:48:45.407
And another thing about it that hurted me so bad, when I came back home I had an uncle,

660
00:48:45.407 --> 00:48:51.003
Dr. Tilden Lemelle, lived about three quarters of a mile from where I was at.

661
00:48:51.003 --> 00:48:55.480
He was a professor at CU and I didn't even know it.

662
00:48:55.480 --> 00:49:03.688
I can call him now he'll say, "Butch, if I'd have known you were there then, I would have came got you."

663
00:49:03.688 --> 00:49:13.015
But I had another part of the story that I don't want to put there as one of the reasons that I came back home.

664
00:49:13.015 --> 00:49:16.000
<v CRAMER>You don't want to talk about it?

665
00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:20.053
<v LEMELLE>I'll talk about it to you all but not on camera.

666
00:49:20.053 --> 00:49:20.421
<v CRAMER>Okay.

667
00:49:20.421 --> 00:49:22.632
<v LEMELLE>My wife knows about it, but . . .

668
00:49:22.632 --> 00:49:25.211
<v CRAMER>We can pause it if you want.

669
00:49:25.211 --> 00:49:27.789
If you want to tell us now.

670
00:49:27.789 --> 00:49:28.158
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

671
00:49:28.158 --> 00:49:30.000
<v CRAMER>Okay. [break in tape]

672
00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.429
<v LEMELLE>I was saying if I would have stayed with my cousin Gwendolyn in

673
00:49:36.429 --> 00:49:49.286
<c.p>Colorado, in Boulder, and got my education there and went on, Barak Obama would have followed me because I know the quality I could have gathered.

674
00:49:49.286 --> 00:49:56.703
Especially having an uncle who was a professor at the college . . . well two of them.

675
00:49:56.703 --> 00:50:03.626
Two brothers, Dr. Wilbert Lemelle and Dr. Tilden Lemelle, they always followed each other.

676
00:50:03.626 --> 00:50:09.560
Dr. Wilbert Lemelle went to CU first and then his brother followed.

677
00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:17.473
And during the time I was there, Tilden was a professor, an English professor, at CU.

678
00:50:17.473 --> 00:50:19.451
Speaks like seven languages.

679
00:50:19.451 --> 00:50:21.923
And he chews me out.

680
00:50:21.923 --> 00:50:29.341
He said, "Butch, man, if I would have known you were that close to me."

681
00:50:29.341 --> 00:50:36.264
He said, "Your uncle would have came got you, and you'd have stayed here."

682
00:50:36.264 --> 00:50:44.670
Because he know what happened, and he used some other words but we won't go into that.

683
00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:50.604
So I know that, but I still came back and loved Mossville.

684
00:50:50.604 --> 00:51:00.000
<v CRAMER>How did it . . . How did living there change you, and did it manifest in your return to Louisiana?

685
00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.201
<v LEMELLE>I guess it's two things that it changed in me.

686
00:51:06.201 --> 00:51:18.603
That part of my life showed me what are the possibilities for a human being, especially a male human being.

687
00:51:18.603 --> 00:51:22.944
There are so many possibilities and opportunities,

688
00:51:22.944 --> 00:51:37.207
but some things can spoil you that you don't put forth one hundred percent effort because you can get by with sixty percent.

689
00:51:37.207 --> 00:51:42.788
So I came back doing sixty percent of effort,

690
00:51:42.788 --> 00:51:55.190
which didn't let me get on the ladder as I should have because I was only putting out sixty percent.

691
00:51:55.190 --> 00:51:58.291
And I'll make somebody smile.

692
00:51:58.291 --> 00:52:18.134
Another great change in my life was that there was a female that came in my life and changed me from being sixty percent to want to be a one hundred percent.

693
00:52:18.134 --> 00:52:34.257
Her name was Leona, and I tell my wife all the time if it was not for her the sixty percent that I was out doing,

694
00:52:34.257 --> 00:52:37.978
forty percent of it was bad.

695
00:52:37.978 --> 00:52:41.698
And I'm being honest about myself.

696
00:52:41.698 --> 00:52:46.659
I was spoiled, and I was a thug.

697
00:52:46.659 --> 00:52:51.000
<v CRAMER>You were seventeen or sixteen, too.

698
00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:52.619
<v LEMELLE>Sixteen, seventeen years old.

699
00:52:52.619 --> 00:52:58.690
I came back home and wanted to be something that I wasn't supposed to be.

700
00:52:58.690 --> 00:53:08.000
So I only gave life sixty percent and forty percent of that is again, I say forty percent of the sixty was bad.

701
00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:09.619
Because I was spoiled.

702
00:53:09.619 --> 00:53:12.048
Wanted to be . . . I don't know.

703
00:53:12.048 --> 00:53:20.143
I didn't say . . . I never thought I wanted to be a nobody, but I was headed to being a nobody.

704
00:53:20.143 --> 00:53:22.571
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You wanted to be a man.

705
00:53:22.571 --> 00:53:26.619
<v LEMELLE>But I wanted to be the wrong kind of man.

706
00:53:26.619 --> 00:53:30.667
I wasn't being a man like I should have been.

707
00:53:30.667 --> 00:53:33.500
<v CRAMER>So tell me about how y'all met.

708
00:53:33.500 --> 00:53:37.143
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You ever heard of a club called The Paradise?

709
00:53:37.143 --> 00:53:41.190
Valery got a club . . . had a club called The Paradise.

710
00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:42.810
I was there working.

711
00:53:42.810 --> 00:53:45.238
Behind the bar I was working.

712
00:53:45.238 --> 00:53:50.095
He'd come messing with me, talking about my eyes, this and that.

713
00:53:50.095 --> 00:53:52.119
He'd love talk to me.

714
00:53:52.119 --> 00:53:55.762
Those are just some of the thing I remember.

715
00:53:55.762 --> 00:54:02.643
<v LEMELLE>I blowed her mind. [Laughs] She wanted me to . . . She’s here after fifty-eight years she's still here.

716
00:54:02.643 --> 00:54:08.714
But seriously this is mine, some of this stuff you don't have to put out,

717
00:54:08.714 --> 00:54:16.405
but I'm telling you all my life being in Mossville and where I come from and what changed me

718
00:54:16.405 --> 00:54:18.429
<c.p>wasn't where I was living.

719
00:54:18.429 --> 00:54:24.500
It’s what I wanted to do different than what I was raised up to do.

720
00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:32.595
We all can go on the wrong path, and I was finding an easy way out because I always said,

721
00:54:32.595 --> 00:54:38.262
"I won't have to do what my dad did in order to make it.

722
00:54:38.262 --> 00:54:40.690
I can do better than that.

723
00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:42.714
I can make mine easy."

724
00:54:42.714 --> 00:54:45.952
Want my hands to stay soft and pretty.

725
00:54:45.952 --> 00:54:46.357
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

726
00:54:46.357 --> 00:54:50.000
I used to say that all the time.

727
00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:54.526
<v LEMELLE>But then someone showed me that there was another way.

728
00:54:54.526 --> 00:54:59.958
That he said in his word, "You don't work, you don't eat."

729
00:54:59.958 --> 00:55:13.084
<v CRAMER>What was the moment when you realized you didn't want to do the forty percent anymore and what role . . . like what moment happened between you and Ms. Leona that . . .

730
00:55:13.084 --> 00:55:16.705
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Girl, you don't want to know that! [Laughs]

731
00:55:16.705 --> 00:55:20.779
<v CRAMER>Chelsea [Arseneault] is blushing all the way over there.

732
00:55:20.779 --> 00:55:24.853
<v LEMELLE>I don't know what she have in her mind.

733
00:55:24.853 --> 00:55:26.663
<v CRAMER>Well I'm imagining you . . .

734
00:55:26.663 --> 00:55:28.474
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Well in my heart . . .

735
00:55:28.474 --> 00:55:28.926
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

736
00:55:28.926 --> 00:55:33.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>. . . there might be something different than in yours.

737
00:55:33.000 --> 00:55:35.686
<v LEMELLE>Well she's asking me or you?

738
00:55:35.686 --> 00:55:37.924
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Well she's asking you. [laughs]

739
00:55:37.924 --> 00:55:46.875
<v CRAMER>I'm just wondering at what moment . . . You know, you always see when it's reenacted somehow where a woman has really . . .

740
00:55:46.875 --> 00:55:50.008
<v LEMELLE>Let me just tell you a story.

741
00:55:50.008 --> 00:55:50.456
<v CRAMER>Yes.

742
00:55:50.456 --> 00:55:54.037
<v LEMELLE>And it's going to be a brief story.

743
00:55:54.037 --> 00:55:56.722
And then you'll see a person.

744
00:55:56.722 --> 00:56:03.436
I don't know what she has in her mind, but I've told her this before.

745
00:56:03.436 --> 00:56:10.598
In every person's life there's somewhere there's some sentimental things in your life, in your heart.

746
00:56:10.598 --> 00:56:12.836
Sentimental can turn to care.

747
00:56:12.836 --> 00:56:15.074
Care can turn to love.

748
00:56:15.074 --> 00:56:21.788
When I met her, like she said where she was and what she was doing.

749
00:56:21.788 --> 00:56:23.578
I talked to her.

750
00:56:23.578 --> 00:56:28.501
About two days later I passed this place, The Paradise Club.

751
00:56:28.501 --> 00:56:34.320
She was sitting out and had a little girl on the front porch.

752
00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:36.110
Her name was Jackie.

753
00:56:36.110 --> 00:56:39.244
And I stopped, and my heart fell.

754
00:56:39.244 --> 00:56:46.853
I said, is this the lady I talked to the other night that was looking so beautiful,

755
00:56:46.853 --> 00:56:52.224
and here she's sitting out with a child that has muscular dystrophy,

756
00:56:52.224 --> 00:56:59.833
that can't do for herself, and she's trying to be out in the world to make a

757
00:56:59.833 --> 00:57:02.518
<c.p>living for herself and this baby?

758
00:57:02.518 --> 00:57:04.309
And this is true.

759
00:57:04.309 --> 00:57:14.156
My sympathy for her through that baby just made me want to be there for her and be there for her kid.

760
00:57:14.156 --> 00:57:16.841
This is where it started at.

761
00:57:16.841 --> 00:57:26.688
And from that day out there on that porch I made it my business to continue to try to communicate with her.

762
00:57:26.688 --> 00:57:32.059
It was not my biological child, but nobody could tell me that.

763
00:57:32.059 --> 00:57:33.850
That was my baby.

764
00:57:33.850 --> 00:57:39.221
This is when I fell in love with her, through her child.

765
00:57:39.221 --> 00:57:54.887
And of course some of . . . The other man still was there lurking that you don't want to really . . . So she decided one day that she was . . . She and her mother and I were real close.

766
00:57:54.887 --> 00:58:02.048
But she decided that she was going to leave because I wasn't working like most guys.

767
00:58:02.048 --> 00:58:06.076
She left, and she went to San Antonio [Texas].

768
00:58:06.076 --> 00:58:07.419
I'm telling them.

769
00:58:07.419 --> 00:58:09.210
You tell them then.

770
00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:11.000
It's your story.

771
00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:13.199
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It's because you wasn't working.

772
00:58:13.199 --> 00:58:15.838
<v LEMELLE>Isn't that what I just said?

773
00:58:15.838 --> 00:58:17.157
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh, I'm sorry.

774
00:58:17.157 --> 00:58:18.037
<v CRAMER>It was.

775
00:58:18.037 --> 00:58:19.356
It's okay, though.

776
00:58:19.356 --> 00:58:20.676
It was sweet.

777
00:58:20.676 --> 00:58:29.472
<v LEMELLE>Because I wasn't working on a permanent big-time job, her brothers and them were probably out hustling on the [?] boat.

778
00:58:29.472 --> 00:58:31.231
That wasn't my thing.

779
00:58:31.231 --> 00:58:33.870
I wanted an easy way out.

780
00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:39.148
So she left . . . left Lake Charles and went to San Antonio, Texas.

781
00:58:39.148 --> 00:58:44.426
Her mother told me, she said, "I think she's in San Antonio.”

782
00:58:44.426 --> 00:58:47.065
My grandmother named Ira Pots Towner.

783
00:58:47.065 --> 00:58:52.343
I went to her and she laid hands on my and prayed.

784
00:58:52.343 --> 00:58:58.060
I had never been to San Antonio, didn't know where San Antonio was

785
00:58:58.060 --> 00:58:58.940
<c.p>or nothing.

786
00:58:58.940 --> 00:59:05.977
I left Lake Charles with seventy dollars my grandmother gave me and she prayed for me.

787
00:59:05.977 --> 00:59:10.375
She said, "You go get her if you want her."

788
00:59:10.375 --> 00:59:14.333
I said, "Grandma I don't know where I'm going."

789
00:59:14.333 --> 00:59:17.852
And you know what my grandmother told me?

790
00:59:17.852 --> 00:59:21.370
"God will lead you if it's for you."

791
00:59:21.370 --> 00:59:31.046
And I left here and I don't know how it was except it was God, that when I got to San Antonio,

792
00:59:31.046 --> 00:59:39.403
Texas and pull over to a little service station and ask the question they said, "Well that's right there."

793
00:59:39.403 --> 00:59:42.042
<v LEONA LEMELLE>God lead you there, huh, baby?

794
00:59:42.042 --> 00:59:44.681
<v LEMELLE>God lead me directly to her.

795
00:59:44.681 --> 00:59:46.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Sure did.

796
00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:49.868
<v LEMELLE>And I gave her a choice: you come home or I'm coming to get my baby.

797
00:59:49.868 --> 00:59:53.011
It's left up to you if you want to come back with us.

798
00:59:53.011 --> 00:59:54.945
I think she beat me to the car.

799
00:59:54.945 --> 00:59:57.604
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You don't remember saying, "Baby, I want you to come home."

800
00:59:57.604 --> 00:59:58.813
<v LEMELLE>No, I don't remember that.

801
00:59:58.813 --> 00:59:59.780
<v LEONA LEMELLE>"I got three jobs."

802
00:59:59.780 --> 01:00:00.505
I remember that.

803
01:00:00.505 --> 01:00:02.440
<v LEMELLE>I don't remember telling you I need you.

804
01:00:02.440 --> 01:00:04.615
I told you I come to get my baby.

805
01:00:04.615 --> 01:00:05.582
Stephanie was a baby.

806
01:00:05.582 --> 01:00:08.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You know I wouldn't let that baby go nowhere.

807
01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:11.871
<v LEMELLE>Because little Jackie . . . What happened to my picture?

808
01:00:11.871 --> 01:00:13.323
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It's up there.

809
01:00:13.323 --> 01:00:15.742
It’s on that white frame.

810
01:00:15.742 --> 01:00:17.677
<v LEMELLE>The one by herself.

811
01:00:17.677 --> 01:00:20.097
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Can you reach it, baby?

812
01:00:20.097 --> 01:00:21.548
Because she tall.

813
01:00:21.548 --> 01:00:25.419
Can you reach that white frame up there?

814
01:00:25.419 --> 01:00:25.903
Yeah.

815
01:00:25.903 --> 01:00:29.290
Or you going to need a stool?

816
01:00:29.290 --> 01:00:32.194
<v LEMELLE>We going to get it later.

817
01:00:32.194 --> 01:00:33.645
You still recording?

818
01:00:33.645 --> 01:00:34.129
<v CRAMER>[Agrees]

819
01:00:34.129 --> 01:00:36.548
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh, Lord [ . . .?] on and on.

820
01:00:36.548 --> 01:00:38.000
It's okay.

821
01:00:38.000 --> 01:00:38.673
<v LEMELLE>And . . .

822
01:00:38.673 --> 01:00:43.383
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I'll get [ . . .?] to fix it for me.

823
01:00:43.383 --> 01:00:45.402
That's the baby.

824
01:00:45.402 --> 01:00:48.766
She was, what, four, five?

825
01:00:48.766 --> 01:00:49.439
Five.

826
01:00:49.439 --> 01:00:54.150
When she died she was almost six.

827
01:00:54.150 --> 01:00:56.168
<v LEMELLE>But guess what?

828
01:00:56.168 --> 01:01:04.243
Jackie was almost six and Leona . . . God has played such a big

829
01:01:04.243 --> 01:01:18.374
<c.p>part in our lives that we may not even look at and realize, but little Jackie died October the second, 1968.

830
01:01:18.374 --> 01:01:27.794
And Stephanie, who she never thought she'd have any more kids, was born November . . .

831
01:01:27.794 --> 01:01:28.467
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Six.

832
01:01:28.467 --> 01:01:31.832
<v LEMELLE>Not even six weeks later.

833
01:01:31.832 --> 01:01:33.850
Four weeks later.

834
01:01:33.850 --> 01:01:42.598
I mean six weeks later Stephanie came into the world, November the nineteenth.

835
01:01:42.598 --> 01:01:50.000
And then we turned around and we had a son.

836
01:01:50.000 --> 01:02:00.909
<v LEONA LEMELLE>But during the time I was pregnant carrying David, I had cancer in my womb and I didn't know until after I delivered him.

837
01:02:00.909 --> 01:02:04.091
So they had to do a hysterectomy.

838
01:02:04.091 --> 01:02:06.364
<v CRAMER>It's a miracle, though, that . . .

839
01:02:06.364 --> 01:02:07.273
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It is.

840
01:02:07.273 --> 01:02:09.091
<v CRAMER>. . . the baby was born.

841
01:02:09.091 --> 01:02:10.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

842
01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:12.222
<v CRAMER>With . . . I mean, that's amazing.

843
01:02:12.222 --> 01:02:14.000
<v LEMELLE>It's all about God.

844
01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:16.667
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I wanted a boy so bad.

845
01:02:16.667 --> 01:02:18.000
So bad.

846
01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:22.338
<v LEMELLE>But if that wouldn't happened we'd probably be like my cousin.

847
01:02:22.338 --> 01:02:25.099
We probably have about sixteen children. [Laughs]

848
01:02:25.099 --> 01:02:25.493
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

849
01:02:25.493 --> 01:02:28.648
I wish I could have had some more.

850
01:02:28.648 --> 01:02:29.042
Yeah.

851
01:02:29.042 --> 01:02:31.408
<v LEMELLE>Because that's the sister of twelve.

852
01:02:31.408 --> 01:02:33.775
So you know how that goes.

853
01:02:33.775 --> 01:02:35.746
<v CRAMER>You were one of six.

854
01:02:35.746 --> 01:02:36.930
<v LEMELLE>One of six.

855
01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:43.239
And that girl walked in here, then we'd have probably still coming from high school. [Laughs]

856
01:02:43.239 --> 01:02:46.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[Laughs] I'm too old for that.

857
01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:49.733
<v CRAMER>So what year did y'all get married?

858
01:02:49.733 --> 01:02:50.800
<v LEMELLE>Ask her.

859
01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:52.933
She if she remembers.

860
01:02:52.933 --> 01:02:56.133
<v CRAMER>Oh wait, forty-seven years ago, right?

861
01:02:56.133 --> 01:02:58.267
Or forty-eight years ago?

862
01:02:58.267 --> 01:02:59.867
<v LEMELLE>June eleventh, 1967.

863
01:02:59.867 --> 01:03:03.600
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I can't keep up with the date.

864
01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:04.667
<v LEMELLE>Clear everything.

865
01:03:04.667 --> 01:03:05.733
Nineteen sixty-seven.

866
01:03:05.733 --> 01:03:10.000
I tell . . . And I've found your poem.

867
01:03:10.000 --> 01:03:11.067
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Where it was?

868
01:03:11.067 --> 01:03:14.622
<v LEMELLE>It's in there in my shirt drawer, my t-shirt drawer.

869
01:03:14.622 --> 01:03:18.178
If you go in there look now you'll see it.

870
01:03:18.178 --> 01:03:19.956
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Y'all want to read it?

871
01:03:19.956 --> 01:03:23.867
<v LEMELLE>I'm going to tell y'all what I did for her birthday.

872
01:03:23.867 --> 01:03:26.356
Almost four years ago for her seventieth.

873
01:03:26.356 --> 01:03:33.111
When she was turning seventy I thought that was something that she needed because we had twenty-five year anniversary.

874
01:03:33.111 --> 01:03:35.600
I told her the sky's the limit.

875
01:03:35.600 --> 01:03:37.378
Whatever you want it's yours.

876
01:03:37.378 --> 01:03:39.156
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Where the socks at, baby?

877
01:03:39.156 --> 01:03:40.578
<v LEMELLE>Where my t-shirts at.

878
01:03:40.578 --> 01:03:42.000
Under the TV.

879
01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:42.679
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh, okay.

880
01:03:42.679 --> 01:03:44.036
<v LEMELLE>All this on camera.

881
01:03:44.036 --> 01:03:46.071
We just common, old common, people.

882
01:03:46.071 --> 01:03:48.446
<v CRAMER>We know where your sock drawer is.

883
01:03:48.446 --> 01:03:50.143
<v LEMELLE>Just leave them down, baby.

884
01:03:50.143 --> 01:03:51.839
We’ll put them back up.

885
01:03:51.839 --> 01:03:52.857
<v CRAMER>Oh my goodness.

886
01:03:52.857 --> 01:03:54.893
What year was this one taken?

887
01:03:54.893 --> 01:03:56.250
That one right there.

888
01:03:56.250 --> 01:03:58.625
<v LEMELLE>That was at a Joe Tex Dance.

889
01:03:58.625 --> 01:04:01.000
Somewhere in the '70s, I guess.

890
01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:03.204
<v CRAMER>So y'all were already married?

891
01:04:03.204 --> 01:04:03.645
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

892
01:04:03.645 --> 01:04:04.086
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

893
01:04:04.086 --> 01:04:08.495
<v LEMELLE>We were out to Joe Tex dance at [Boss?] Auditorium.

894
01:04:08.495 --> 01:04:17.312
And that was a little small, small and I had it taken off it and had it put on there.

895
01:04:17.312 --> 01:04:19.075
<v CRAMER>That's in good condition.

896
01:04:19.075 --> 01:04:22.602
I mean there’s good color quality and everything.

897
01:04:22.602 --> 01:04:25.688
So Becca got good pictures of those.

898
01:04:25.688 --> 01:04:30.097
<v LEMELLE>She's supposed to take and get someone to write this.

899
01:04:30.097 --> 01:04:31.419
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, redo it.

900
01:04:31.419 --> 01:04:35.828
<v LEMELLE>Print it or something and put it in a frame.

901
01:04:35.828 --> 01:04:40.237
But I'm going to read it to you guys about . . .

902
01:04:40.237 --> 01:04:42.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Thank you, honey.

903
01:04:42.000 --> 01:04:44.934
<v LEMELLE>This was after forty-four years together.

904
01:04:44.934 --> 01:04:50.803
On her seventieth birthday I was supposed to sing her a song,

905
01:04:50.803 --> 01:04:58.629
but I didn't sing because someone else sung the song that I was going to sing.

906
01:04:58.629 --> 01:05:03.520
So what I did, I wrote her a quick poem.

907
01:05:03.520 --> 01:05:09.878
And you have to know that before I read the poem to you.

908
01:05:09.878 --> 01:05:13.790
<v LEONA LEMELLE>He just did that out of his head.

909
01:05:13.790 --> 01:05:26.017
<v LEMELLE>That I explained to those that after forty-four years, but before then she was born and raised in Palmetto, Louisiana which is out by Opelousas.

910
01:05:26.017 --> 01:05:31.397
So this is a poem to the woman that I love.

911
01:05:31.397 --> 01:05:40.690
"When you were a little girl I saw you in my dream, the prettiest little girl I'd ever seen.

912
01:05:40.690 --> 01:05:47.537
You grew up some miles from me, not knowing what the future would be.

913
01:05:47.537 --> 01:05:58.297
Then one warm April day that sweet little girl came my way. [Laughs] And there were few words that I could say.

914
01:05:58.297 --> 01:06:04.166
After looking into your big brown eyes, loving you was no surprise.

915
01:06:04.166 --> 01:06:11.502
Now many days has passed away, but I can still tell you I love you.

916
01:06:11.502 --> 01:06:12.480
Happy birthday."

917
01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:13.948
Don't you cry.

918
01:06:13.948 --> 01:06:14.926
<v CRAMER>That's sweet.

919
01:06:14.926 --> 01:06:16.393
I love it.

920
01:06:16.393 --> 01:06:23.240
<v LEMELLE>My spelling may not be the best, but I knew what I was saying.

921
01:06:23.240 --> 01:06:25.197
So where we at?

922
01:06:25.197 --> 01:06:27.642
We still doing oral history?

923
01:06:27.642 --> 01:06:28.620
<v CRAMER>We are.

924
01:06:28.620 --> 01:06:34.000
Hey, Leona is a big part of who you are.

925
01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:34.659
<v LEMELLE>She is.

926
01:06:34.659 --> 01:06:38.284
<v CRAMER>And you always talk about her, so I think that was…

927
01:06:38.284 --> 01:06:40.591
<v LEMELLE>She is a wonderful part of me.

928
01:06:40.591 --> 01:06:45.205
<v CRAMER>With your birthday coming up and your forty-eighth anniversary around a couple months away.

929
01:06:45.205 --> 01:06:45.534
<v LEONA LEMELLE>June.

930
01:06:45.534 --> 01:06:50.807
<v CRAMER>So we can't not . . . I mean if you don't want to answer this question that's fine,

931
01:06:50.807 --> 01:06:53.773
but when did you know you were in love?

932
01:06:53.773 --> 01:06:55.750
Y'all are a very romantic couple.

933
01:06:55.750 --> 01:06:57.727
I don't usually ask this question.

934
01:06:57.727 --> 01:06:59.045
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It's kind of embarrassing.

935
01:06:59.045 --> 01:07:01.352
<v CRAMER>You don't want to talk about it?

936
01:07:01.352 --> 01:07:02.011
That's fine.

937
01:07:02.011 --> 01:07:03.000
That's fine.

938
01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:05.794
<v LEMELLE>Are you ashamed of me?

939
01:07:05.794 --> 01:07:06.353
<v LEONA LEMELLE>No.

940
01:07:06.353 --> 01:07:10.265
<v CRAMER>She just . . . I'm asking a prying question.

941
01:07:10.265 --> 01:07:10.824
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

942
01:07:10.824 --> 01:07:22.000
<v CRAMER>I thought, well you guys are very romantic maybe we'd get a little tape, but we don't have to.

943
01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:24.108
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I could tell you the reason.

944
01:07:24.108 --> 01:07:30.081
One reason I fell in love with Butch because he wasn’t ashamed to kiss me in public.

945
01:07:30.081 --> 01:07:34.297
He wasn't ashamed to tell people he was in love with me.

946
01:07:34.297 --> 01:07:37.108
I had never had that done before, okay?

947
01:07:37.108 --> 01:07:39.568
And like, he wasn't ashamed of me.

948
01:07:39.568 --> 01:07:41.324
I was part of him.

949
01:07:41.324 --> 01:07:44.486
And then got a little bit closer and closer.

950
01:07:44.486 --> 01:07:49.757
And when I found out that part, that it that was kind of good too.

951
01:07:49.757 --> 01:07:53.270
Now that's about as far as I'm going with that.

952
01:07:53.270 --> 01:07:56.432
<v CRAMER>Everybody has a love . . . You know what I mean?

953
01:07:56.432 --> 01:08:01.351
Everybody has that moment in their life when their soulmate, they have that moment.

954
01:08:01.351 --> 01:08:01.703
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

955
01:08:01.703 --> 01:08:06.270
Sometimes I have to go back to that to make my memories better.

956
01:08:06.270 --> 01:08:08.378
Just think about the good times.

957
01:08:08.378 --> 01:08:12.595
We did have good times, and we take off and go places.

958
01:08:12.595 --> 01:08:19.973
You had a surgery on your knee or something, and we was in Moss Bluff and I went to pick you

959
01:08:19.973 --> 01:08:21.730
<c.p>up at your mama house.

960
01:08:21.730 --> 01:08:23.486
I never forget that part.

961
01:08:23.486 --> 01:08:27.703
And yeah, I'm thinking about the broke leg or whatever you had,

962
01:08:27.703 --> 01:08:32.973
and you and me was trying to do something and . . . But we did it. [Laughs]

963
01:08:32.973 --> 01:08:35.081
<v LEMELLE>That was the Barry White days.

964
01:08:35.081 --> 01:08:37.892
"I can't get enough of your love, baby."

965
01:08:37.892 --> 01:08:40.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[laughs] Yeah, that was good.

966
01:08:40.000 --> 01:08:42.728
<v LEMELLE>Now you see what married to her.

967
01:08:42.728 --> 01:08:43.897
She's Barry White.

968
01:08:43.897 --> 01:08:45.845
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I ain’t no Barry White.

969
01:08:45.845 --> 01:08:48.962
<v LEMELLE>Well you make me feel like Barry White.

970
01:08:48.962 --> 01:08:49.742
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh God.

971
01:08:49.742 --> 01:08:50.911
<v LEMELLE>Now she's tickled.

972
01:08:50.911 --> 01:08:55.197
She y'all got to take some of that off of there.

973
01:08:55.197 --> 01:08:57.925
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, please don't put that on there.

974
01:08:57.925 --> 01:09:00.263
<v CRAMER>[laughs] But you know what though?

975
01:09:00.263 --> 01:09:02.211
Y'all will probably love it.

976
01:09:02.211 --> 01:09:04.939
We'll keep it on there for you.

977
01:09:04.939 --> 01:09:05.718
<v LEMELLE>For us.

978
01:09:05.718 --> 01:09:06.108
<v CRAMER>Yes.

979
01:09:06.108 --> 01:09:06.498
<v LEMELLE>Right.

980
01:09:06.498 --> 01:09:10.784
<v CRAMER>We'll keep it on there for you, and you'll be tickled.

981
01:09:10.784 --> 01:09:18.188
<v LEMELLE>But I do want you to know this day that I yet still love my wife and it's still,

982
01:09:18.188 --> 01:09:22.474
I'll tell anybody, a heart of love for people in general.

983
01:09:22.474 --> 01:09:26.761
And a heart of feelings can lead you to something great.

984
01:09:26.761 --> 01:09:29.099
And I still say to today,

985
01:09:29.099 --> 01:09:38.061
if it was not been for little Jackie I probably would have tried to hit and run and went on about my business.

986
01:09:38.061 --> 01:09:40.789
But it didn't turn out that way.

987
01:09:40.789 --> 01:09:42.737
You know what I've done?

988
01:09:42.737 --> 01:09:45.854
To show you where God brought us from.

989
01:09:45.854 --> 01:09:55.207
We were living in Port Arthur in a little old . . . Trying so hard to hustle to show that I cared and I loved her.

990
01:09:55.207 --> 01:09:58.324
We were living in a place that was . . .

991
01:09:58.324 --> 01:09:59.103
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Two rooms.

992
01:09:59.103 --> 01:09:59.883
<v LEMELLE>Two rooms.

993
01:09:59.883 --> 01:10:03.000
No, it was really one big room.

994
01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:06.714
<v LEONA LEMELLE>The bedroom and the kitchen was on the other side.

995
01:10:06.714 --> 01:10:10.429
<v LEMELLE>On one . . . And we had to go down the hall.

996
01:10:10.429 --> 01:10:10.800
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

997
01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:11.914
<v LEMELLE>To the bathroom.

998
01:10:11.914 --> 01:10:12.657
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yes, Lord.

999
01:10:12.657 --> 01:10:13.771
Other people [ . . .?] too.

1000
01:10:13.771 --> 01:10:21.943
<v LEMELLE>And there was this lady and her little baby, my baby, that I was working in Orange and living in Port Arthur.

1001
01:10:21.943 --> 01:10:26.400
Now if you don't think this is love, something wrong with you.

1002
01:10:26.400 --> 01:10:34.943
From off of Highway 90 at Livingston Shipyard I had to get home to her and that baby and then have a way.

1003
01:10:34.943 --> 01:10:35.686
I walked.

1004
01:10:35.686 --> 01:10:39.771
I started walking at 11:05 from that site to Port Arthur.

1005
01:10:39.771 --> 01:10:42.000
Across the Port Arthur bridge.

1006
01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:44.933
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You ever been on the Port Arthur bridge?

1007
01:10:44.933 --> 01:10:47.867
<v LEMELLE>Single . . . At that time it was two-way traffic.

1008
01:10:47.867 --> 01:10:58.500
I'm walking on the spot about like this to get home to her and that baby because I was afraid what could happen to them with me not there.

1009
01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:02.167
I could have easily just said, "Heck away with it.

1010
01:11:02.167 --> 01:11:02.533
Whatever."

1011
01:11:02.533 --> 01:11:04.000
<v CRAMER>Eleven at night?

1012
01:11:04.000 --> 01:11:04.818
<v LEMELLE>Eleven at night.

1013
01:11:04.818 --> 01:11:09.182
I got home from eleven until about one . . . close to two o'clock that morning I think.

1014
01:11:09.182 --> 01:11:10.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I don't remember.

1015
01:11:10.000 --> 01:11:12.455
<v CRAMER>And then turned around and went back to work?

1016
01:11:12.455 --> 01:11:13.273
<v LEONA LEMELLE>The next day.

1017
01:11:13.273 --> 01:11:14.091
<v LEMELLE>The next day.

1018
01:11:14.091 --> 01:11:15.182
I was working evenings.

1019
01:11:15.182 --> 01:11:20.909
But I was able to get to her and get to my baby and see that they were okay and secure.

1020
01:11:20.909 --> 01:11:22.273
I say, "Well it's okay.

1021
01:11:22.273 --> 01:11:26.636
We'll make it one more day here, but after a while God going to bless us."

1022
01:11:26.636 --> 01:11:28.000
And sure enough we moved.

1023
01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:31.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Found a little house with two rooms and a bathroom.

1024
01:11:31.000 --> 01:11:32.014
<v LEMELLE>Found another house.

1025
01:11:32.014 --> 01:11:33.028
<v LEONA LEMELLE>With a bathroom.

1026
01:11:33.028 --> 01:11:34.380
<v LEMELLE>With a bathroom inside.

1027
01:11:34.380 --> 01:11:39.113
That was only the first thing we could find temporary because it's . . . you know.

1028
01:11:39.113 --> 01:11:41.141
And we moved from there to . . .

1029
01:11:41.141 --> 01:11:43.169
<v CRAMER>What year . . . About when was that?

1030
01:11:43.169 --> 01:11:46.211
Was that around that picture time in the '70s?

1031
01:11:46.211 --> 01:11:47.901
<v LEMELLE>No, that was before that.

1032
01:11:47.901 --> 01:11:49.592
That was well before that.

1033
01:11:49.592 --> 01:11:50.606
<v CRAMER>Sixty-eight, sixty-nine, maybe?

1034
01:11:50.606 --> 01:11:51.620
<v LEMELLE>Let me see.

1035
01:11:51.620 --> 01:11:52.972
<v CRAMER>You got married in'67?

1036
01:11:52.972 --> 01:11:55.000
<v LEMELLE>That was somewhere in '69.

1037
01:11:55.000 --> 01:11:57.667
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Because David was born in '60 . . . In '70 . . .

1038
01:11:57.667 --> 01:11:59.333
<v LEMELLE>No, this was before then.

1039
01:11:59.333 --> 01:12:01.000
This had to be '67.

1040
01:12:01.000 --> 01:12:02.000
That was '67.

1041
01:12:02.000 --> 01:12:04.333
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, we ain't even had Stefanie yet.

1042
01:12:04.333 --> 01:12:07.333
<v LEMELLE>The Port Arthur was the early parts of '60.

1043
01:12:07.333 --> 01:12:08.667
When we first met.

1044
01:12:08.667 --> 01:12:13.000
She told me I had to get a job or she was stepping.

1045
01:12:13.000 --> 01:12:16.333
So the best thing I could find was Livingston Shipyard.

1046
01:12:16.333 --> 01:12:18.000
I went to work there.

1047
01:12:18.000 --> 01:12:20.000
<v CRAMER>And what'd you do there?

1048
01:12:20.000 --> 01:12:21.234
<v LEMELLE>I was a maintenance.

1049
01:12:21.234 --> 01:12:26.477
<v CRAMER>Was that your first job or did you have work before that in high school and stuff?

1050
01:12:26.477 --> 01:12:28.327
<v LEMELLE>I guess my first real job.

1051
01:12:28.327 --> 01:12:31.103
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I can tell you what he used to do.

1052
01:12:31.103 --> 01:12:35.729
Sell clothes at S&amp;M store on [Ryan?] Street that used to be on Third Street.

1053
01:12:35.729 --> 01:12:36.654
<v LEMELLE>That was for . . .

1054
01:12:36.654 --> 01:12:38.505
<v CRAMER>Like in high school or something?

1055
01:12:38.505 --> 01:12:40.972
<v LEONA LEMELLE>He didn’t want to get his fingers dirty.

1056
01:12:40.972 --> 01:12:41.280
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

1057
01:12:41.280 --> 01:12:43.748
<v CRAMER>No one wants to get their fingers dirty.

1058
01:12:43.748 --> 01:12:46.523
<v LEONA LEMELLE>And I done dug sweet potatoes, pulled sugarcane down.

1059
01:12:46.523 --> 01:12:47.757
I done it all.

1060
01:12:47.757 --> 01:12:50.841
<v LEMELLE>Nobody tell you to be born in Opelousas and Palmetto.

1061
01:12:50.841 --> 01:12:53.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I was born in Deridder. [Laughs]

1062
01:12:53.000 --> 01:12:54.214
<v LEMELLE>But raised there.

1063
01:12:54.214 --> 01:13:00.286
<v CRAMER>So well we have about . . . I'm going to say we have about ten more minutes.

1064
01:13:00.286 --> 01:13:01.500
<v LEMELLE>Take all the . . .

1065
01:13:01.500 --> 01:13:07.571
<v CRAMER>And one of my questions for you was what was your first job, so that's . . .

1066
01:13:07.571 --> 01:13:08.786
<v LEMELLE>My first job?

1067
01:13:08.786 --> 01:13:10.000
<v CRAMER>Going back.

1068
01:13:10.000 --> 01:13:18.873
<v LEMELLE>My first job, really paid job was working with my father in a private home.

1069
01:13:18.873 --> 01:13:19.465
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

1070
01:13:19.465 --> 01:13:26.563
<v LEMELLE>That was my first real job that I really loved and enjoyed.

1071
01:13:26.563 --> 01:13:28.930
<v CRAMER>What did you do?

1072
01:13:28.930 --> 01:13:31.296
<v LEMELLE>I was a custodian.

1073
01:13:31.296 --> 01:13:34.254
Custodian, part-time chef, part-time chauffeur.

1074
01:13:34.254 --> 01:13:37.803
Name it, that's what we did.

1075
01:13:37.803 --> 01:13:38.394
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

1076
01:13:38.394 --> 01:13:44.310
<v LEMELLE>And before you leave I want to show you something.

1077
01:13:44.310 --> 01:13:48.451
I don't want to get up with

1078
01:13:48.451 --> 01:13:50.225
<c.p>this on me.

1079
01:13:50.225 --> 01:13:52.000
<v CRAMER>You're tethered.

1080
01:13:52.000 --> 01:13:54.133
<v LEMELLE>Because she'll get upset.

1081
01:13:54.133 --> 01:13:55.733
<v CRAMER>To the camera.

1082
01:13:55.733 --> 01:13:58.400
So how old were you?

1083
01:13:58.400 --> 01:13:58.933
<v LEMELLE>Sixteen.

1084
01:13:58.933 --> 01:13:59.467
Seventeen?

1085
01:13:59.467 --> 01:14:03.733
That was after I came back from Colorado.

1086
01:14:03.733 --> 01:14:04.267
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1087
01:14:04.267 --> 01:14:07.467
So did your dad pay you?

1088
01:14:07.467 --> 01:14:08.000
<v LEMELLE>Yes.

1089
01:14:08.000 --> 01:14:09.600
They paid me.

1090
01:14:09.600 --> 01:14:16.000
<v CRAMER>You were working for your dad or with you dad or . . .?

1091
01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:17.787
<v LEMELLE>I was working with my dad.

1092
01:14:17.787 --> 01:14:18.681
<v CRAMER>With your dad.

1093
01:14:18.681 --> 01:14:19.574
<v LEMELLE>For these people.

1094
01:14:19.574 --> 01:14:22.851
And you want to ask the question did they pay me?

1095
01:14:22.851 --> 01:14:24.043
Yes they paid me.

1096
01:14:24.043 --> 01:14:24.936
And guess what?

1097
01:14:24.936 --> 01:14:31.191
My father told me, "You going to learn how to save, and when you get paid, you give me your money."

1098
01:14:31.191 --> 01:14:35.362
And I said, "I don't see why I got to give you my money.

1099
01:14:35.362 --> 01:14:35.660
I'm

1100
01:14:35.660 --> 01:14:36.553
<c.p>working for it."

1101
01:14:36.553 --> 01:14:36.851
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Sassy!

1102
01:14:36.851 --> 01:14:38.638
<v LEMELLE>You see this scar right here?

1103
01:14:38.638 --> 01:14:40.128
Can you still see it?

1104
01:14:40.128 --> 01:14:41.021
It's grown up.

1105
01:14:41.021 --> 01:14:43.404
Do you see a scar on my nose?

1106
01:14:43.404 --> 01:14:44.000
<v CRAMER>Yes.

1107
01:14:44.000 --> 01:14:47.229
<v LEMELLE>That's my daddy's left hand with his wedding band.

1108
01:14:47.229 --> 01:14:47.587
Pow.

1109
01:14:47.587 --> 01:14:53.686
"Don't you ever tell me you going to do what you want to do with your money."

1110
01:14:53.686 --> 01:14:54.404
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That's right.

1111
01:14:54.404 --> 01:14:56.197
<v LEMELLE>I fell out for dead.

1112
01:14:56.197 --> 01:14:57.632
But I got up.

1113
01:14:57.632 --> 01:14:59.785
Came there Friday for my check.

1114
01:14:59.785 --> 01:15:02.296
I told them, "I don't want it.

1115
01:15:02.296 --> 01:15:03.372
You keep it.

1116
01:15:03.372 --> 01:15:05.166
I don't want it, Daddy.

1117
01:15:05.166 --> 01:15:05.883
I promise.

1118
01:15:05.883 --> 01:15:07.318
You just . . ." Said, "Okay.

1119
01:15:07.318 --> 01:15:11.982
I'm going to take your money, and I'll give you some of it.

1120
01:15:11.982 --> 01:15:14.852
So when school starts you'll have some money."

1121
01:15:14.852 --> 01:15:17.722
My father would give me an allowance out.

1122
01:15:17.722 --> 01:15:22.744
And he said, "All I want to do is keep some of your money.

1123
01:15:22.744 --> 01:15:24.897
I'm going to put it up."

1124
01:15:24.897 --> 01:15:31.354
He would keep like ten or twenty dollars, but the rest he said, "You do what you want."

1125
01:15:31.354 --> 01:15:34.224
And he would put it in his Bible.

1126
01:15:34.224 --> 01:15:38.170
And I was keeping up calculating what he had for me.

1127
01:15:38.170 --> 01:15:46.422
When it came time for me to get ready to go do my school shopping if I was supposed to have 130 dollars,

1128
01:15:46.422 --> 01:15:50.726
I had 260 because every dollar I put, he added to it.

1129
01:15:50.726 --> 01:15:53.596
He said, "Now this is how you save.

1130
01:15:53.596 --> 01:15:55.031
Say, "Thank you, Daddy."

1131
01:15:55.031 --> 01:15:56.466
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I was alright now.

1132
01:15:56.466 --> 01:15:59.695
<v LEMELLE>“No, now this is not yours to go spend.

1133
01:15:59.695 --> 01:16:04.000
We going to the bank and open up you an account."

1134
01:16:04.000 --> 01:16:05.368
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Who coming in there?

1135
01:16:05.368 --> 01:16:07.079
<v CRAMER>Hi, we're still doing it.

1136
01:16:07.079 --> 01:16:07.763
It's okay.

1137
01:16:07.763 --> 01:16:08.447
It's fine.

1138
01:16:08.447 --> 01:16:10.842
We can pause it. [break in tape]

1139
01:16:10.842 --> 01:16:12.553
<v CRAMER>You know what I mean?

1140
01:16:12.553 --> 01:16:14.947
I'm not going to ask it again.

1141
01:16:14.947 --> 01:16:19.053
I'm not going to ask you what your first job was again.

1142
01:16:19.053 --> 01:16:19.395
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

1143
01:16:19.395 --> 01:16:21.105
<v CRAMER>You know what I mean?

1144
01:16:21.105 --> 01:16:21.789
<v LEMELLE>You promise?

1145
01:16:21.789 --> 01:16:24.526
<v CRAMER>[laughs] Unless you want to tell it again.

1146
01:16:24.526 --> 01:16:27.605
But so some of these you've already talked about.

1147
01:16:27.605 --> 01:16:37.868
I think next time what we'll do is pick up talking about one more question I had for you to finish talking about was describing Big Mama's house, the grounds.

1148
01:16:37.868 --> 01:16:43.000
The garden, the chicken house, the smoke house, the pigs, that kind of thing.

1149
01:16:43.000 --> 01:16:53.515
<v LEMELLE>I saw a picture yesterday at Della's of my dad out in the backyard with a hog that he named, called Son.

1150
01:16:53.515 --> 01:16:53.993
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Son?

1151
01:16:53.993 --> 01:16:58.294
<v LEMELLE>And the hog stood every bit of this high.

1152
01:16:58.294 --> 01:17:02.596
Della had . . . We been going through some old pictures,

1153
01:17:02.596 --> 01:17:10.243
and Della have the picture of my dad out in the back in his pig pen.

1154
01:17:10.243 --> 01:17:11.199
Our backyard.

1155
01:17:11.199 --> 01:17:15.978
I'm going to get it and show it to you.

1156
01:17:15.978 --> 01:17:16.456
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1157
01:17:16.456 --> 01:17:17.412
Oh yes.

1158
01:17:17.412 --> 01:17:23.147
You might want to see if Ms. Susan will scan it, too.

1159
01:17:23.147 --> 01:17:25.537
<v LEMELLE>That was way back there.

1160
01:17:25.537 --> 01:17:26.493
<v CRAMER>Oh wow.

1161
01:17:26.493 --> 01:17:29.838
What year do you think that was?

1162
01:17:29.838 --> 01:17:31.750
Fifty, maybe, or '55?

1163
01:17:31.750 --> 01:17:32.228
<v LEMELLE>Fifty-five.

1164
01:17:32.228 --> 01:17:32.706
Fifty-two.

1165
01:17:32.706 --> 01:17:33.184
Fifty-five.

1166
01:17:33.184 --> 01:17:33.662
<v CRAMER>Wow.

1167
01:17:33.662 --> 01:17:35.574
<v LEMELLE>Let me see, '46.

1168
01:17:35.574 --> 01:17:37.963
I was born in '46.

1169
01:17:37.963 --> 01:17:40.353
So '56 I was ten.

1170
01:17:40.353 --> 01:17:40.831
Fifty-six.

1171
01:17:40.831 --> 01:17:41.309
Fifty-seven.

1172
01:17:41.309 --> 01:17:48.000
Old enough for me to be out there remembering riding on the hog.

1173
01:17:48.000 --> 01:17:50.254
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You would ride on the hog?

1174
01:17:50.254 --> 01:17:54.011
<v LEMELLE>See, my wife find out some things she didn't know.

1175
01:17:54.011 --> 01:17:55.889
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, I'm learning a lot.

1176
01:17:55.889 --> 01:18:00.772
<v LEMELLE>We used to . . . My dad had a hog and his name was Son.

1177
01:18:00.772 --> 01:18:04.905
A big old black hog, and we could actually ride him.

1178
01:18:04.905 --> 01:18:06.407
He was their pet.

1179
01:18:06.407 --> 01:18:09.037
And my dad would talk to him.

1180
01:18:09.037 --> 01:18:10.915
He had a little stick.

1181
01:18:10.915 --> 01:18:12.794
He would, "Son, come on."

1182
01:18:12.794 --> 01:18:17.302
Whatever my dad told that hog to do he would do it.

1183
01:18:17.302 --> 01:18:19.180
He'd let us ride him.

1184
01:18:19.180 --> 01:18:21.434
Della got a picture of him.

1185
01:18:21.434 --> 01:18:25.566
I saw it yesterday, and I'm going to get that picture.

1186
01:18:25.566 --> 01:18:29.323
And we going to have the picture of my dad.

1187
01:18:29.323 --> 01:18:33.079
And somewhere I had a picture here of my grandfather.

1188
01:18:33.079 --> 01:18:36.085
Grandfather Duff Towner standing out with his shovel.

1189
01:18:36.085 --> 01:18:43.222
I saw that picture somewhere and I put them all away and I forgot where I put it at.

1190
01:18:43.222 --> 01:18:51.111
But my grandfather on the Towner side used to . . . You remember I was telling you about the coal the man made?

1191
01:18:51.111 --> 01:18:57.122
Well I got a picture of him somewhere standing out with his shovel in his hand.

1192
01:18:57.122 --> 01:18:59.000
Standing up with it.

1193
01:18:59.000 --> 01:19:03.062
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I want to know what Paw-Paw did with that hog.

1194
01:19:03.062 --> 01:19:03.875
<v CRAMER>Me, too.

1195
01:19:03.875 --> 01:19:05.094
<v LEMELLE>We butchered him.

1196
01:19:05.094 --> 01:19:06.312
We ate it.

1197
01:19:06.312 --> 01:19:09.156
That's what everybody did with their animals.

1198
01:19:09.156 --> 01:19:11.188
No use telling a tale.

1199
01:19:11.188 --> 01:19:14.844
It taken about seven good men to butcher him.

1200
01:19:14.844 --> 01:19:22.156
They had to get an old neighbor across the street, old Mr. Ben Hartman, to get his horse.

1201
01:19:22.156 --> 01:19:24.594
And I'll never forget we cried.

1202
01:19:24.594 --> 01:19:28.250
We cried, but it was time to butcher him.

1203
01:19:28.250 --> 01:19:35.156
And they had to get this man with a horse and come over when they butchered him.

1204
01:19:35.156 --> 01:19:40.031
And they had to put a thing way up in the tree.

1205
01:19:40.031 --> 01:19:42.875
And had the horse to have to

1206
01:19:42.875 --> 01:19:47.344
<c.p>hoist him up for them to . . . That's how big he was.

1207
01:19:47.344 --> 01:19:48.156
<v CRAMER>You cried?

1208
01:19:48.156 --> 01:19:49.375
<v LEMELLE>Big old hog.

1209
01:19:49.375 --> 01:19:51.000
<v CRAMER>You were sad?

1210
01:19:51.000 --> 01:19:51.746
<v LEMELLE>Oh, yeah.

1211
01:19:51.746 --> 01:19:53.239
It was our pet.

1212
01:19:53.239 --> 01:19:56.225
But Daddy say, “I raised him to eat."

1213
01:19:56.225 --> 01:19:58.092
Oh, she about to cry.

1214
01:19:58.092 --> 01:20:00.704
I'm going to bring you that picture.

1215
01:20:00.704 --> 01:20:02.197
I'm about to holler.

1216
01:20:02.197 --> 01:20:03.317
That's Mike. [Laughs]

1217
01:20:03.317 --> 01:20:07.796
<v CRAMER>Well that will be a really good place to start next time.

1218
01:20:07.796 --> 01:20:15.261
And when we talk about Big Mama's house and then we'll continue on with . . . We've talked a lot about community.

1219
01:20:15.261 --> 01:20:18.246
We've talked about World War II last time.

1220
01:20:18.246 --> 01:20:21.232
We've talked about some of your childhood memories.

1221
01:20:21.232 --> 01:20:30.563
We've mentioned a little bit about entertainment, but we'll kind of come back to that, and some medical treatment, and some places, especially in Mossville,

1222
01:20:30.563 --> 01:20:38.401
that are just no longer there so we can kind of help paint that . . . Keep that picture painted for the history.

1223
01:20:38.401 --> 01:20:43.254
And then in the meantime we'll see you on your birthday next week.

1224
01:20:43.254 --> 01:20:44.000
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

1225
01:20:44.000 --> 01:20:52.626
<v CRAMER>And on behalf of the center and on behalf of ICM [Imperial Calcasieu Museum] and on behalf of the whole project I just want to thank you for giving the time today.

1226
01:20:52.626 --> 01:20:54.243
<c.p>And thank you for sitting here . . .

1227
01:20:54.243 --> 01:20:55.861
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Y'all going to meet back here?

1228
01:20:55.861 --> 01:20:56.130
<v CRAMER>Sure.

1229
01:20:56.130 --> 01:20:57.478
Is that okay with you?

1230
01:20:57.478 --> 01:20:58.826
Is that okay with y'all?

1231
01:20:58.826 --> 01:20:59.096
<v LEMELLE>When?

1232
01:20:59.096 --> 01:20:59.904
After the fourth?

1233
01:20:59.904 --> 01:21:00.983
After our next meeting?

1234
01:21:00.983 --> 01:21:04.757
<v CRAMER>Maybe not after the next meeting because I might have to teach that day.

1235
01:21:04.757 --> 01:21:07.991
It would probably be the following week or I know you guys . . .

1236
01:21:07.991 --> 01:21:08.261
<v LEMELLE>February.

1237
01:21:08.261 --> 01:21:10.687
<v CRAMER>Y'all are going on a bed and breakfast trip.

1238
01:21:10.687 --> 01:21:11.765
You can cut it.

1239
01:21:11.765 --> 01:21:15.000
And I know y'all are going on a bed and breakfast . . .

1240
01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:15.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4557.

1241
01:21:15.429 --> 01:21:15.857
End Session III.]
